course: dictionary
lesson: 1736
title: Clamp: a tool and a grip
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Clamp is a word with two jobs. It can be a thing, a tool that holds two things tightly together. It can also be a doing word, meaning to grip something firmly. Context tells you which one. «In science a clamp on a stand holds test tubes steady» shows the tool. «He clamped his hand over his mouth» shows the doing word.
explain: Ask what the word does in the sentence. Is it a thing, like a tool, or an action, like gripping? Then try your meaning in the sentence. If «He held his hand over his mouth» still makes sense, then clamp means hold tightly, and the word tightly is the clue.
explain: Clamp also appears in phrases. «The school is clamping down on phones» does not mean that someone is squeezing phones. Clamp down on means to act strongly to stop something, so the school has made a strict rule. Look at the words that come after clamp to find the phrase's meaning.
fact: A clamp is a tool that holds two things tightly together, or holds something firmly in place, often by turning a screw.
fact: Clamp as a doing word means to hold something tightly or to grip firmly, as in «He clamped his hand over his mouth.»
fact: «Clamp down on» means to act strongly to stop something, as in «The school is clamping down on phones.»
fact: In science, a clamp on a stand holds test tubes or burettes steady.
fact: A wheel clamp is a metal lock on a car's wheel, so the car cannot be driven. It is a punishment for bad parking.
exercise: «Jake grabs a crab; it clamps his thumb until his sister dips his hand back in the water.» What does clamps mean here? | Waves about | Tickles softly | Lets go gently | *Grips tightly and will not let go
exercise: What does «clamping down on» mean in «The school is clamping down on phones»? | *Makes a strict rule to stop them | Sells new phones | Lets phones be used freely | Forgets about phones
exercise: «Scaffolding is a frame of poles and boards clamped together by hand.» What does clamped mean? | Thrown away in a pile | Painted bright colours | Left spread far apart | *Held tightly together
test: In «a clamp on a stand holds test tubes steady», what kind of word is clamp? | *A thing, a tool that holds things tightly in place | A doing word, meaning to shout | A kind of animal | A kind of glue
test: Why might someone have a wheel clamp fixed to their car? | To keep the car warm | To make the car go faster | *To stop the car being driven, as a punishment for bad parking | To wash the wheels
exam: Which sentence uses clamp as a doing word meaning to grip firmly? | The wheel clamp was bright yellow. | *He clamped his hand over his mouth. | The clamps are in the science cupboard. | The clamp on the stand was made of steel.
